senses.gloss:
Māheśvarasūtras
(‘aphorisms of महेश्वर [Śiva]’)
------------------------------------------
Grammar is the science of any language. Greater its perfection, more refined will be the language.
Indologists, especially those who are adepts in the सन्स्क्रित् लन्गुअगे, have opined that the Aṣṭādhyāyī of पाणिनि (350 B. C.) is the best grammar that any language has produced.
Hindu tradition believes that the letters of the Sanskrit alphabet were given by Lord महेश्वर or शिव himself to पाणिनि by playing on his डमरु (small drum) fourteen times. Thus were created the fourteen sūtras known as the Māheśvarasūtras embodying all the alphabets.
These fourteen sūtras are:
a-i-uṇ;
ṛ-ḷr-k;
e-oṅ;
ai-au-c;
ha-ya-va-ra-ṭ;
la-ṇ;
ñ-ma-ṅ-ṇa-na-m;
jha-bha-ñ;
gha-ḍha-dha-ṣ;
ja-ba-ga-ḍa-da-ś;
kha-pha-cha-ṭha-tha-ca-ṭa-ta-v;
ka-pa-y;
śa-ṣa-sa-r;
She is पार्वती, the feminine counterpart of महेश्वर शिव. She is shown as fair-complexioned, with three eyes and four arms. She wears a crown of hair (jaṭā-मुकुट) and is bedecked with all ornaments. She carries the battle-axe (paraśu) and the deer (mṛga) in two hands and shows the hand-poses of protection (abhaya) and giving boons (varada) with the other two. She rides on a bull (Nandi).
